<article class="env-definition" id="030E">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/030E">Definition <span data-tag="030E">9.26.1</span>.</a> Let $K/k$ be a field extension. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
A collection of elements $\{ x_ i\} _{i \in I}$ of $K$ is called <em>algebraically independent</em> over $k$ if the map 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  k[X_ i; i\in I] \longrightarrow K  \]
</div>
<p>
 which maps $X_ i$ to $x_ i$ is injective. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The field of fractions of a polynomial ring $k[x_ i; i \in I]$ is denoted $k(x_ i; i\in I)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>purely transcendental extension</em> of $k$ is any field extension $K/k$ isomorphic to the field of fractions of a polynomial ring over $k$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>transcendence basis</em> of $K/k$ is a collection of elements $\{ x_ i\} _{i \in I}$ which are algebraically independent over $k$ and such that the extension $K/k(x_ i; i\in I)$ is algebraic. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>